    <section class="section-shell science-section" id="body-fit-and-anthropometry-in-sport">
      <h2 class="section-title">Body Fit and Anthropometry in Sport</h2>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        <strong>Anthropometry</strong> refers to measurable aspects of the human body—height, limb lengths, segment ratios, muscle mass, and body composition.
      </p>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        These characteristics shape both performance potential and how “natural” or efficient an activity feels.
      </p>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">Key Insights</h3>
      <ul class="science-list">
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Mechanical advantage:</strong> Body proportions determine leverage and force transfer. Shorter limbs improve mechanical advantage in power or strength-based movements, while longer limbs enhance reach and efficiency in endurance or stroke-based sports.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/somatotyping/5D073F87003D9E4A8B4360B9E6FAF58B" target="_blank">Carter, J.E.L. &amp; Heath, B.H. (1990). <em>Somatotyping—Development and Applications.</em> Cambridge University Press.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/026404101750158341" target="_blank">Bourgois, J. et al. (2001). Anthropometric characteristics of elite athletes across 22 sports. <em>Journal of Sports Sciences, 19(7)</em>, 477–489.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.2165/00007256-200131110-00003" target="_blank">Norton, K. &amp; Olds, T. (2001). Morphological evolution of athletes over the 20th century. <em>Sports Medicine, 31(11)</em>, 763–783.</a></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Energy cost and efficiency:</strong> Certain body shapes reduce metabolic cost for specific movements—lighter, more linear builds favor endurance; denser or more muscular builds favor explosive tasks. The same level of effort can therefore produce very different results depending on biomechanical alignment.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.2165/11597140-000000000-00000" target="_blank">Ackland, T.R., Lohman, T.G., Sundgot-Borgen, J. et al. (2012). Current status of body composition assessment in sport. <em>Sports Medicine, 42(3)</em>, 227–249.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2014.0994" target="_blank">Sedeaud, A., Marc, A., et al. (2014). Secular trends in body dimensions of elite athletes. <em>Proceedings of the Royal Society B, 281(1788)</em>, 20140994.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://sportsmedicine-open.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s40798-023-00523-9" target="_blank">Władysław, K. et al. (2023). Somatotype profiles across modern sports: A scoping review. <em>Sports Medicine Open, 9(1)</em>, 18.</a></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Selection and specialization:</strong> Talent identification and long-term athlete development models use anthropometric benchmarks to match individuals to optimal sports. Even recreationally, people self-select into activities where their body type “feels right,” which supports success and enjoyment.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span><a href="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/026404101750158341" target="_blank">Bourgois, J. et al. (2001). <em>Journal of Sports Sciences, 19(7)</em>, 477–489.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40279-014-0159-5" target="_blank">Bridge, C.A., Santos, J.F., Chaabène, H. et al. (2014). Physical and physiological profiles of taekwondo athletes: a systematic review. <em>Sports Medicine, 44(6)</em>, 713–733.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://journals.lww.com/acsm-csmr/Fulltext/2010/11000/Early_Sport_Specialization__Roots,_Effectiveness,.7.aspx" target="_blank">Malina, R.M. (2010). Early sport specialization: roots, effectiveness, risks. <em>Current Sports Medicine Reports, 9(6)</em>, 364–371.</a></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Psychological relevance:</strong> When body mechanics align with a sport’s demands, learning accelerates, discomfort decreases, and the feedback loop between competence and enjoyment strengthens—regardless of competitive level.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.2165/11597140-000000000-00000" target="_blank">Ackland et al. (2012). <em>Sports Medicine</em>.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://sportsmedicine-open.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s40798-023-00523-9" target="_blank">Władysław et al. (2023). <em>Sports Medicine Open</em>.</a></span>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">In summary</h3>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        <strong>Body–sport fit isn’t about perfection.</strong> It is about recognising that structure interacts with physical demands so people can find sports that feel natural, reduce injury risk, and support long-term motivation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-shell science-section" id="why-enjoying-a-sport-and-feeling-good-at-it-matters-for-adults">
      <h2 class="section-title">Why Enjoying a Sport — and Feeling Good at It — Matters for Adults</h2>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        Doing a sport you <em>enjoy</em> and <em>feel competent</em> in yields measurable benefits for adult mental health, motivation, and life satisfaction.
      </p>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        Effects are modest but consistently observed across large datasets and systematic reviews.
      </p>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">Key Evidence-Based Insights</h3>
      <ul class="science-list">
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Enjoyment predicts long-term adherence:</strong> Higher enjoyment during exercise strongly predicts continued participation months or years later.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1469029214002163" target="_blank">Rhodes, R.E. &amp; Kates, A. (2015). Can the affective response to exercise predict future behaviour? <em>Psychology of Sport and Exercise, 16</em>, 56–63.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://ijbnpa.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1479-5868-9-78" target="_blank">Teixeira, P.J., Carraça, E.V., Markland, D., Silva, M.N., &amp; Ryan, R.M. (2012). Exercise, physical activity, and self-determination theory: a systematic review. <em>Int J Behav Nutr Phys Act, 9</em>, 78.</a></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Perceived competence boosts enjoyment and persistence:</strong> Feeling capable increases intrinsic motivation and adherence; this “competence → enjoyment → adherence” pathway is central in Self-Determination Theory.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span><a href="https://books.google.com/books/about/Self_Determination_Theory.html?id=pv0qDwAAQBAJ" target="_blank">Ryan, R.M. &amp; Deci, E.L. (2017). <em>Self-Determination Theory: Basic Psychological Needs in Motivation, Development, and Wellness.</em> Guilford Press.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://ijbnpa.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1479-5868-9-78" target="_blank">Teixeira et al. (2012). <em>Int J Behav Nutr Phys Act</em>.</a></span>
            <span><a href="https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-psych-010419-050715" target="_blank">Ntoumanis, N., Ng, J.Y.Y., Prestwich, A., et al. (2021). A meta-analysis of self-determination theory in physical activity and sport. <em>Annual Review of Psychology, 72</em>, 109–133.</a></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Sport participation and mental health:</strong> Regular participation correlates with lower anxiety and depression symptoms, with group or organised settings often yielding the largest benefits.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span><a href="https://www.ajpmonline.org/article/S0749-3797(15)00163-9/fulltext" target="_blank">Rebar, A.L., Stanton, R., Geard, D., Short, C., Duncan, M.J., &amp; Vandelanotte, C. (2015). A meta-analysis of the effect of physical exercise on depression and anxiety in non-clinical adults. <em>American Journal of Preventive Medicine, 49(5)</em>, 649–662.</a></span>
